{
 "id": 324,
 "created_at": "2026-11-12T22:32:34.548492+05:30",
 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DfkPhNpPTPd/",
 "title": "3 Criteria for a Successful Business Idea",
 "topic": "business ideas",
 "summary": "To generate a viable business idea, it must meet three specific criteria: passion, utility, and profitability. Combining these elements helps ensure that an idea is both sustainable for the founder and valuable to the market.",
 "key_points": [
  "Passion: Choose something you enjoy doing long-term or are genuinely interested in learning about. This keeps you motivated during challenging times.",
  "Expertise: You do not need to be an expert to start; passion helps you persist even if you are not currently one.",
  "Utility: Your product or service must solve a real need. There must be a sufficient 'Total Addressable Market' (TAM) of people willing to engage with what you are offering.",
  "Profitability: The idea needs to have the potential to make money. This does not strictly mean selling directly to consumers; you can also monetize through brands or companies paying for access to your distribution or audience."
 ],
 "content": "How to come up with business ideas? It needs to have three very distinct features. Feature number one, it's something that you should be really passionate about. It's either something that you love doing, have done for a long time, or it's something you're really interested in learning about. You don't actually have to have domain knowledge. You could be an engineer who wants to build a legal business. Passion is so important because it'll carry you through two things. You may or may not be an expert, and that's okay. And number two, the bad days. What's next? It actually needs to be something that is useful in the world. I will explain this term in a second. Let's say you're really passionate, for some reason, about making braids out of nose hair. Is it useful in the world? And more importantly, there's not a large enough market for it. Is there an addressable market? Enough people who are willing to care about this thing? That is your TAM or your Total Addressable Market. The people who care don't have to be your parents or your siblings or your friends. But so long as someone in the world cares about that thing and there's enough people in the world who care about that thing, then you have a market. The third most important thing is... Any guesses? It has to be something that can make money. That doesn't mean you have to sell things directly to people. For example, I have almost 2 million followers across platforms, but none of them are paying me to watch or consume the content. Instead, there's enough brands and companies in the world who are willing to pay to access this following or this distribution. So long as somebody else is paying, your business will run. Well, congratulations! That's a business idea. And I'd love to hear yours in the comments.",
